	'domain-management-services'   => array(
		'kw'          => 'domain management',
		'lede'        => 'Domain management keeps your names renewed, secure and pointing where they should, so a lapsed registration never takes your website or email offline.',
		'h2'          => 'Domain management: what it covers and why it matters',
		'body'        => array(
			'Every domain is rented by the year from a registrar. <strong>Domain management</strong> is the routine work that keeps those registrations in order: renewals, contact details, DNS records, locks and a record of who owns each name.',
			'It matters most to businesses that hold more than a handful of names, or whose website and email depend on one name that nobody has looked at since it was registered.',
			'Many of these problems stay invisible until the day a website or inbox stops working. By then the fix is urgent, and sometimes impossible if the name has been released and registered by someone else.',
			array( 'h3' => 'The core tasks of domain management' ),
			array(
				'<strong>Renewals:</strong> auto-renew turned on, with a payment card that will not expire before the renewal date.',
				'<strong>Contact details:</strong> a registrant email address that someone reads, on a domain the business controls.',
				'<strong>Security:</strong> registrar lock enabled and two-factor authentication on the registrar account.',
				'<strong>DNS:</strong> records for the website and email documented, with changes made by a named person.',
				'<strong>Consolidation:</strong> names spread across several registrars brought into one account where practical.',
			),
			array( 'h3' => 'Common domain management failures' ),
			'The most common failure is an expired name. Registrars send renewal notices to the registrant email address. If that address belongs to a former employee or an agency that has moved on, the notices go unread.',
			'The second is lost control: a name registered in a staff member&rsquo;s personal account, or by a web designer, with no written record of who owns it. Recovering it can take weeks and sometimes a legal claim.',
			'A third is a rushed transfer. A name cannot move to a new registrar within 60 days of registration or of a previous transfer, so leaving a move until the last week before renewal rarely works.',
			'Each failure has the same cause: nobody owns the job. One responsible person, a written list of names with their expiry dates and a review twice a year prevent most of them.',
			array( 'h3' => 'How Domain Brothers helps' ),
			'Good domain management starts with an inventory. We review where each name is registered, who the contacts are and when each one expires, then agree the changes with you in writing. Nothing starts and nothing is charged until you have said yes.',
			'When a name needs to move, read our <a href="/domain-transfer/">domain transfer</a> guide and the <a href="/epp-guidelines/">EPP code guidelines</a>. To add names to your portfolio, browse <a href="/all-domains/">all domains</a>.',
			'Questions can go through the <a href="/contact-us/">contact page</a>. A person replies within one business day.',
		),
		'steps'       => 'company',
		'steps_title' => 'How to get started, in four steps',
		'link'        => array( 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domain_name_registrar', 'the Wikipedia article on domain name registrars', 'For background on how registrars hold and renew names, read' ),
	),

